exists su name module manuscript hook prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing the hook for a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write exactly one short spoken line.
- This is the 0 to 3 second hook.
- Keep it between 6 and 8 words.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Match the source topic.
- Prefer urgency, clarity, and curiosity over vagueness.
- Focus on tension or truth, not setup or summary..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript fit prompt ob text quoted.text.You rewrite spoken teaching manuscript sections so they fit a strict word-count band while preserving role, meaning, and clarity.

Task:
- Rewrite the supplied draft.
- Obey TARGET_WORDS exactly.
- Aim for GOAL_WORDS when possible.
- Preserve the same stage role and main claims.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Do not add unrelated new concepts.
- Prefer tightening or compressing before expanding.
- Obey FEEDBACK when it is present.
- Return only the rewritten section..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript promise prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing the promise segment for a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 3 to 12 second promise.
- Keep it between 18 and 24 words.
- Make a clear promise about what the listener will understand, see, or be able to notice by the end of the full teaching arc.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Stay grounded in the source.
- Speak like a narrator, not a list.
- The promise should describe the outcome of all three segments together, not just the next section..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript roles prompt ob text quoted.text.You extract a three-stage teaching arc from source text.

Task:
- Write exactly 3 lines.
- Line 1 is Segment 1 and must establish.
- Line 2 is Segment 2 and must reveal.
- Line 3 is Segment 3 and must expand.

Requirements:
- Segment 1: define the core idea, system, or situation; explain the basic mechanism; imply one simple concrete example.
- Segment 2: expose the hidden layer, misunderstanding, dependency, tension, or contradiction; explain the deeper mechanism; sharpen or correct the first understanding.
- Segment 3: show the consequence, implication, application, outcome, or larger meaning; connect the idea to lived reality, behaviour, systems, or future stakes.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- One line per segment role.
- No numbering, no labels, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Each line should be 8 to 18 words.
- Stay grounded in the source..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript promise fit prompt ob text quoted.text.You rewrite a spoken teaching promise so it fits a strict word-count band without losing its meaning.

Task:
- Rewrite the draft promise.
- Keep it between 18 and 24 words.
- Preserve the same core outcome and spoken tone.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Do not add new claims.
- Prefer tightening over expanding.
- Return one clean sentence only..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript roadmap prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing the roadmap segment for a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 12 to 20 second roadmap.
- Keep it between 16 and 20 words.
- Tell the listener what the manuscript will cover in sequence.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Keep the roadmap concrete and easy to follow.
- Do not repeat the hook or promise wording.
- The roadmap must mirror the three segment roles in order: establish, reveal, expand..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript segment one prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing Segment 1 of a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 20 to 60 second segment.
- Keep it between 85 and 100 words.
- This segment establishes the system.
- Its job is to give the listener a stable mental model.

Rules:
- The hook already defines the topic, so do not re-introduce the topic from scratch.
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Use complete sentences.
- Ground the segment in the source text.
- Build naturally from the roadmap.

Internal structure:
- Light orientation.
- Clear claim.
- Basic mechanism.
- Simple concrete example.
- Small immediate implication.

Must cover:
- define the system clearly
- explain the basic mechanism
- give a simple example

What it should feel like:
- Okay, I understand what this is.

What to avoid:
- deep complexity
- big conclusions
- abstract language
- hidden contradictions
- jumping to large consequences..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript segment two prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing Segment 2 of a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 60 to 105 second segment.
- Keep it between 95 and 115 words.
- This segment reveals the hidden layer.
- Its job is to expose something non-obvious or counterintuitive.

Rules:
- The hook already defines the topic, so do not re-introduce the topic from scratch.
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Use complete sentences.
- Ground the segment in the source text.
- Add new substance rather than repeating Segment 1.

Internal structure:
- Minimal orientation.
- Strong claim.
- Deeper mechanism.
- More specific example.
- Stronger implication.

Must cover:
- expose a false or incomplete understanding
- explain the deeper or hidden mechanism
- sharpen the contrast with Segment 1

What it should feel like:
- Wait, I did not realize that.

What to avoid:
- repeating segment 1
- surface-level explanation
- repeating Segment 1 phrasing..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript segment three prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing Segment 3 of a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 105 to 150 second segment.
- Keep it between 95 and 115 words.
- This segment expands into the consequence layer.
- Its job is to show what follows from the earlier explanation and why the listener should care.

Rules:
- The hook already defines the topic, so do not re-introduce the topic from scratch.
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Use complete sentences.
- Ground the segment in the source text.
- Complete the main teaching arc with clear progression.

Internal structure:
- Very fast setup.
- Clear consequence.
- System-level mechanism.
- Broader real-world scenario.
- Strong implication.

Must cover:
- show the consequence or outcome
- scale the idea outward
- connect it to lived reality

What it should feel like:
- Oh, this actually matters.

What to avoid:
- re-explaining mechanics
- staying small-scale
- introducing new unrelated concepts..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript recap prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing the recap for a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 150 to 170 second recap.
- Keep it between 40 and 50 words.
- Summarize the core lesson in a compact way by compressing Segment 1, Segment 2, and Segment 3 into one clear truth.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Do not introduce new major ideas.
- Make the recap feel earned and clarifying..text.quoted be text ya

exists su name module manuscript cta prompt ob text quoted.text.You are writing the call to action for a long-form teaching manuscript.

Task:
- Write the 170 to 180 second CTA.
- Keep it between 20 and 25 words.
- End with a clear, practical invitation or response that extends or embodies the insight.

Rules:
- Plain prose only.
- No labels, no bullets, no markdown, no surrounding quotes.
- Keep it actionable, human, and concise.
- Fit naturally after the recap..text.quoted be text ya

